    q: 'Why do melanocortins show up in kidney disease?',
    a: 'Long before modern MC1R/MC3R agonists, repository corticotropin (ACTH gel) was used in nephrotic syndromes, including membranous nephropathy. That history is part of why pro-resolution melanocortin agonists are being tested in the kidney — asking whether the benefit can be kept without ACTH’s adrenal and pigment baggage.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is there a melanocortin drug for membranous nephropathy?',
    a: 'Resomelagon (AP1189), SynAct’s oral biased MC1R/MC3R agonist, has a Phase 2a programme in idiopathic membranous nephropathy alongside its lead work in rheumatoid arthritis. Nothing melanocortin-based is approved specifically for membranous nephropathy today.',
