  ]}
>
  <KeyTakeaways
    items={[
      'Membranous nephropathy is an autoimmune glomerular disease that causes heavy proteinuria and nephrotic syndrome when antibodies attack the kidney’s filtration barrier.',
      'Repository corticotropin (ACTH gel) has a long, imperfect clinical history in proteinuric nephropathies — a clue that melanocortin receptors on immune and kidney cells matter beyond cortisol.',
      'Resomelagon (AP1189) is an oral biased MC1R/MC3R agonist in Phase 2a for idiopathic membranous nephropathy, built to drive resolution signalling without MC2R/cortisol or pigment-linked cAMP.',
      'The kidney programme is earlier and more speculative than resomelagon’s rheumatoid-arthritis lead; no melanocortin drug is approved for membranous nephropathy.',
      'This page is the condition-side entry to a story the site already tells on /resomelagon and /therapeutics#corticotropin.',
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    Membranous nephropathy is where an old melanocortin drug and a new one meet in the kidney.
    The disease is autoimmune injury to the glomerular filter; the historical tool was
    repository <a href="/acth">corticotropin</a>; the modern attempt is a biased agonist that
    tries to keep melanocortin’s resolving effects without ACTH’s adrenal cost.<Cite n={3} /><Cite n={1} />
  </p>

  <h2>What breaks</h2>
  <p>
    In membranous nephropathy, autoantibodies (most often against PLA2R on podocytes) deposit
    along the glomerular basement membrane. The filtration barrier leaks protein — often enough
    to cause nephrotic-range proteinuria, oedema and a higher risk of clotting and infection.
    Treatment today runs through supportive care, immunosuppression and, increasingly,
    B-cell–targeted biologics; it is effective for many patients and hard for others, which is
    why alternative immune-modulating pathways stay interesting.
  </p>

  <h2>The ACTH clue</h2>
  <p>
    Long before anyone spoke of biased MC1R/MC3R agonists, clinicians used repository
    corticotropin gel in difficult proteinuric diseases, including membranous nephropathy.
    Some of that effect is cortisol, but a persistent theme in the literature is that ACTH’s
    benefit in the kidney is not fully explained by steroids alone — melanocortin receptors on
    immune cells and resident kidney cells are part of the proposed mechanism.<Cite n={3} /><Cite n={4} />
    That is the historical bridge from the <a href="/therapeutics">therapeutics</a> corticotropin
    story to today’s resolution pharmacology.
  </p>

  <h2>What the pipeline is doing</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/resomelagon">Resomelagon</a> (AP1189) is SynAct’s oral, once-daily biased agonist
    selective for <a href="/receptors/mc1r">MC1R</a> and <a href="/receptors/mc3r">MC3R</a> on
    immune cells. In the defining laboratory work it drives the ERK1/2 arm tied to
    pro-resolution macrophage behaviour while leaving the cAMP arm — and therefore melanogenesis
    — largely untouched, and it ignores MC2R so it does not raise cortisol the way ACTH
    does.<Cite n={2} /> SynAct has taken that molecule into a Phase 2a study in idiopathic
    membranous nephropathy alongside its lead rheumatoid-arthritis programme and earlier
    host-directed antiviral work.<Cite n={1} />
  </p>
  <p>
    The honest framing matches the drug page: the kidney indication is earlier and more
    speculative than the arthritis programme, which itself posted a mixed Phase 2b ADVANCE
    readout in 2026. Membranous nephropathy is a test of whether “one resolution switch, many
    inflamed organs” holds — not a claim that it already does.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    Membranous nephropathy earns a page because it links three things the site already tracks:
    corticotropin’s odd durability in glomerular disease, MC1R/MC3R resolution biology, and a
    live biased-agonist programme. For the molecule see <a href="/resomelagon">resomelagon</a>;
    for neighbouring organ bets see <a href="/ulcerative-colitis">ulcerative colitis</a>,{' '}
    <a href="/systemic-sclerosis">systemic sclerosis</a>, and{' '}
    <a href="/inflammation">the inflammation axis</a>.
  </p>

